<Project>

  <!-- Chain to a parent Directory.Build.props when one exists (in-tree build, e.g.
       Broiler.Browser, which checks this component out as a submodule). Skipped for
       standalone builds. The path is captured in a property first so the inner quotes
       don't collide with the Condition. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerParentBuildProps>$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))</BroilerParentBuildProps>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(BroilerParentBuildProps)" Condition="'$(BroilerParentBuildProps)' != ''" />

  <!--
    THE CONFIGURATION MAPPING, VENDORED.

    Debug-VM/Release-VM are this component's own build types: they select
    Broiler.HtmlBridge.Scripting.Vm and the Broiler.JSeal.Vm package it references over the
    Broiler.JS pair, so the names have to decompose here whether or not a parent repository is
    above us. The imported file carries its own guards — $(BroilerConfigurationMappingApplied)
    for the whole mapping and '$(BroilerBaseConfiguration)' == '' for the decomposition —
    so when a parent has already applied it (an in-tree Broiler.Browser build, which
    imports the identical file from its own eng/), this import does nothing.

    Vendored rather than reached through the parent because a standalone
    `dotnet build Broiler.HtmlBridge.slnx -c Release-VM` must compile OPTIMISED. Without
    the mapping the SDK keys Optimize off `Configuration == 'Release'`, so that build
    succeeds and is wrong with nothing to say so.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.Configurations.props" />

  <!-- Broiler NuGet packaging metadata (vendored; do not edit by hand). -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.Packaging.props" />

  <!-- Repository-owned packaging overrides. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Broiler-Platform/Broiler.HtmlBridge</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageTags>broiler;html;control;webview;dom;javascript;embedding</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    THE TWO DIAGNOSTICS THIS COMPONENT PROMOTES TO ERRORS.

    Both are one defect seen from two sides, and both are about JsValue — the JSEAL
    handle provided by the Broiler.JSeal package. It is a STRUCT, and the
    engine-typed storage it replaced (JSObject, JSValue) was a class. What each reader
    of such a field does:

        f is null              error CS0037    loud
        f is not { }           error CS8518    loud
        f == null              warning CS8073  ALWAYS FALSE
        f != null              warning CS8073  ALWAYS TRUE
        ReferenceEquals(f, g)  warning CA2013  boxes; false forever
        f is { } x             nothing at all  always true, silently

    The bottom three are why this block exists, and being warnings is no safety net:
    a warning is a line of green log in every workflow here.

    CS8073 is the worse of the two promoted, because BOTH its spellings fail open.
    `f == null` is a bail-out guard that stops bailing out; `f != null` is a
    proceed-only-if-present guard that always proceeds. An absent handle is
    JsValue.Missing, never null, so a test that used to mean "absent" comes to mean
    nothing and the code runs on with an empty handle.

    THE SHAPE NEITHER OF THEM SEES: `f is { } x` on a struct is legal, always true, and
    carries no diagnostic at all. There is nothing to promote, so a site that has to know
    whether a handle is absent tests IsMissing and coalesces explicitly.

    Nor does CA2013 see JsValue.ObjectIdentity, which is declared `object?`: passing it
    to ReferenceEquals compares two REFERENCE types, so the rule is blind by
    construction, and it answers TRUE for any two non-object handles because
    ObjectIdentity is null for every one of them. The reads that exist all null-guard
    first.

    CS0472 is the same diagnostic for a BUILT-IN value type (`i == null` on an int). No
    handle is a built-in, so it guards nothing here and is deliberately not promoted.

    Both ids are declared in Broiler.Browser's root Directory.Build.props as well, for the
    rest of that tree. Appending an id twice is harmless, so an in-tree build carrying both
    declarations is intended, not a duplicate to clean up.
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA2013;CS8073</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Inherited noise, kept only so that a STANDALONE build is readable: listing these ids
    means the standalone build reports what the in-tree build does rather than burying a
    real diagnostic under warnings the in-tree build never showed.

    The nullable family is deliberately NOT suppressed here. It is enforced everywhere
    except Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom, which narrows it in its own csproj for the duration of
    that project's migration. That list may shrink and may not grow.

    CS9113 covers primary-constructor parameters kept for API shape. CA1416 is the Windows-only
    surface the canvas rasteriser reaches through Broiler.Graphics. CA2255 is the ModuleInitializer
    advisory: a JSEAL provider registers itself from one, which is the advanced scenario the
    rule's own documentation points at, and a provider nothing has named must register before
    anything asks the registry. SYSLIB0013 is Uri.EscapeUriString, which implements JavaScript's
    encodeURI() and matches its semantics more closely than Uri.EscapeDataString does.

    Each is a candidate to remove per project as that project is cleaned; none of them may
    grow, because that is what the promotion above is for.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS9113;CA1416;CA2255;SYSLIB0013</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
